---
title: How to create a Terraform module
date: 2026-07-31
excerpt: A standard file layout and naming convention for Terraform modules, plus a local setup to scaffold new ones in seconds.
tags: [Terraform, IaC]
---

> This post lays out the convention I use to create a proper Terraform module.

## Why

Writing a Terraform module is a frequent task, so it's worth having it follow a standard.

## How

The goal is to respect the following convention:

```
work-folder
|-- MODULE_NAME
    |- .gitignore
    |- .pre-commit-config.yaml
    |- .terraform.lock.hcl
    |- CHANGELOG.md
    |- CONTRIBUTING.md
    |- LICENSE
    |- main.tf
    |- outputs.tf
    |- providers.tf
    |- README.md
    |- variables.tf
    |- versions.tf
```

## Naming and content conventions

### Module name

When creating a module, it should respect the following convention:

`terraform-PROVIDER-ExplicitName`

### `.gitignore`

This file should be generated by the gitignore online tool: [gitignore.io](https://www.toptal.com/developers/gitignore).

### `.pre-commit-config.yaml`

This file should be the same across all modules, to make sure every module follows the same coding standard.

> `tfsec` was archived by Aqua Security and folded into `trivy` — use the `terraform_trivy` hook rather than the now-deprecated `terraform_tfsec`.

```yaml
---
repos:
  - repo: https://github.com/pre-commit/pre-commit-hooks
    rev: v3.4.0
    hooks:
      - id: check-added-large-files
      - id: check-json
      - id: pretty-format-json
      - id: check-yaml
      - id: end-of-file-fixer
      - id: forbid-new-submodules
      - id: trailing-whitespace
  - repo: https://github.com/jumanjihouse/pre-commit-hook-yamlfmt
    rev: 0.1.0
    hooks:
      - id: yamlfmt
        args: [--mapping, '2', --sequence, '4', --offset, '2']
  - repo: https://github.com/antonbabenko/pre-commit-terraform
    rev: v1.48.0
    hooks:
      - id: terraform_fmt
      - id: terraform_validate
      - id: terraform_tflint
      - id: terraform_trivy
      - id: terraform_docs
  - repo: https://github.com/thlorenz/doctoc
    rev: v2.0.0
    hooks:
      - id: doctoc
```

### `.terraform.lock.hcl`

This file is auto-generated when the `terraform init` command is run.

Whether to commit it here depends on what kind of repo this is. For a **root** configuration you actually run `apply` against, commit it — that's what pins your team to the exact provider versions you tested. For a **reusable** module like the one this post is about, avoid committing it: it only pins the module's own dev environment, and doesn't do anything useful for consumers, who resolve their own lock file against their own `required_providers` constraints anyway.

### `CHANGELOG.md`

This file has to be updated for each tagged version and explain the different updates / features shipped.

**Guiding principles**

- Changelogs are *for humans*, not machines.
- There should be an entry for every single version.
- The same types of changes should be grouped.
- Versions and sections should be linkable.
- The latest version comes first.
- The release date of each version is displayed.
- Mention whether you follow [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/).

**Types of changes**

- `Added` for new features.
- `Changed` for changes in existing functionality.
- `Deprecated` for soon-to-be removed features.
- `Removed` for now removed features.
- `Fixed` for any bug fixes.
- `Security` in case of vulnerabilities.

This one can be a good starting example: [CHANGELOG.md](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/juampynr/4c18214a8eb554084e21d6e288a18a2c/raw/6d61b1ced1c66349cf9ef6ce5eb84546ebf6e79d/CHANGELOG.md).

### `CONTRIBUTING.md`

This file explains how to collaborate on updating the module.

The following one can be a good starting example: [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/PurpleBooth/b24679402957c63ec426/raw/5c4f62c1e50c1e6654e76e873aba3df2b0cdeea2/Good-CONTRIBUTING.md-template.md).

### `LICENSE`

This file protects the intellectual property of the module — but pick one that actually fits the context. If you're publishing a module publicly, a permissive license like [Apache 2.0](https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt) or [MIT](https://opensource.org/license/mit) is what most of the Terraform module ecosystem uses (see the `terraform-aws-modules` org, for example); GPL's copyleft terms are rarely what you actually want for infrastructure code meant to be reused. For internal or client work, you often don't want an open-source license at all — a private repo plus your contract's IP terms may be all that's needed.

### `main.tf`

This file should contain only the main module resources.

```hcl
# Single resource created
resource "ResourceType" "this" {
  ...
}

# Multiple resources created
resource "ResourceType" "these" {
  for_each = ...

  ...
}
```

### `outputs.tf`

This file should contain only the outputs of the main module resources.

```hcl
output "this_ResourceName_ResourceAttribute" {
  description = ""
  value = ...
}
```

### `providers.tf`

This file should contain the provider configuration only.

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}
```

### `README.md`

This file has to be clear about what the module does and how.

Use the `doctoc` command and `terraform-docs` to keep it consistent.

### `variables.tf`

This file should contain only the variables for the module.

```hcl
variable "this_ResourceType" {
  description = ""
  type        = ...
}

variable "these_ResourceType" {
  description = ""
  type        = ...
}
```

### `versions.tf`

This file should contain the required version configuration for the module.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 0.14"

  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 3.31.0"
    }
  }
}
```

## Local set-up configuration

### Retrieve the pre-commit config file locally

```bash
touch ~/.pre-commit-config.yaml
vim ~/.pre-commit-config.yaml
```

### Add a new alias in your `.zshrc`

```bash
function terraform_new_module {
  mkdir $1
  cd $1/
  git init
  curl https://www.toptal.com/developers/gitignore/api/terraform,macos > .gitignore
  cat <<EOT > README.md
# $1

<!-- BEGINNING OF PRE-COMMIT-TERRAFORM DOCS HOOK -->

<!-- END OF PRE-COMMIT-TERRAFORM DOCS HOOK -->
EOT
  echo '# Should contain only main module resources' > main.tf
  touch variables.tf
  touch outputs.tf
  touch versions.tf
  touch providers.tf
  curl https://gist.githubusercontent.com/juampynr/4c18214a8eb554084e21d6e288a18a2c/raw/6d61b1ced1c66349cf9ef6ce5eb84546ebf6e79d/CHANGELOG.md > CHANGELOG.md
  curl https://gist.githubusercontent.com/PurpleBooth/b24679402957c63ec426/raw/5c4f62c1e50c1e6654e76e873aba3df2b0cdeea2/Good-CONTRIBUTING.md-template.md > CONTRIBUTING.md
  # Pick a LICENSE that fits the context — see the note above. Not curled
  # automatically here, since the right choice depends on the module.

  cat ~/tf-pre-commit.yaml > .pre-commit-config.yaml
  pre-commit install
  git add . && pre-commit run --all-files
  git add . && git commit -m 'feat: add initial module files'
}
alias tfn="terraform_new_module"
```

### Usage

```bash
cd client_folder/
tfn terraform-aws-organization
```
